From: ErsatzRS maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] avformat/concat: keep reopened finite input clocks local

A repeated finite HTTP programme URL can return a shorter input on the next
playlist loop. Its duration must not inherit the previous input's maximum DTS.
Reset that input-local high-water value after a successful fresh open.

A global zero-seek which just opened a non-seekable first input is already at
its beginning. Preserve the probe's buffered initial packets instead of seeking
again and dropping the start. Explicit inpoints have already been applied by
open_file. Keep per-stream, nonzero, existing-context and normal seekable paths.

Application integration still requires complete codec/track initialization,
strict delivery/transport checks and platform/package qualification. No source
clock tolerance, readrate, encoder or stream-map change is introduced here.
---
--- a/libavformat/concatdec.c
+++ b/libavformat/concatdec.c
@@ -378,6 +378,9 @@
         av_dict_free(&options);
     }
     cat->cur_file = file;
+    // A reopened live URL can describe a different finite programme.
+    // Derive its duration from this input, not a previous input's DTS maximum.
+    file->next_dts = AV_NOPTS_VALUE;
     file->start_time = !fileno ? 0 :
                        cat->files[fileno - 1].start_time +
                        cat->files[fileno - 1].duration;
@@ -899,6 +902,11 @@
     if (cat->cur_file != &cat->files[left]) {
         if ((ret = open_file(avf, left)) < 0)
             return ret;
+        // Fresh non-seekable input is already positioned at this file's
+        // beginning (including its inpoint). Keep its buffered probe packets.
+        if (stream == -1 && ts == 0 && min_ts <= 0 && max_ts >= 0 && cat->avf->pb &&
+            !(cat->avf->pb->seekable & AVIO_SEEKABLE_NORMAL))
+            return 0;
     } else {
         cat->avf = cur_avf;
     }
